{"post_stream":{"posts":[{"id":272254,"name":"Zac Hatfield-Dodds","username":"Zac-HD","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/zac-hd/{size}/607_2.png","created_at":"2025-09-25T17:54:46.405Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eHi all - after \u003ca href=\"https://discuss.python.org/t/92939\"\u003esome earlier discussion\u003c/a\u003e, I’m pleased to present PEP 806 for discussion.  It’s in most ways a very small proposal, which would make a surprisingly large difference for my code!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFeedback, questions, requests for clarification, etc. all most welcome.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003ch2\u003e\u003ca name=\"p-272254-abstract-1\" class=\"anchor\" href=\"#p-272254-abstract-1\" aria-label=\"Heading link\"\u003e\u003c/a\u003eAbstract\u003c/h2\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePython allows the \u003ccode\u003ewith\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003easync with\u003c/code\u003e statements to handle multiple context managers in a single statement, so long as they are all respectively synchronous or asynchronous. When mixing synchronous and asynchronous context managers, developers must use deeply nested statements or use risky workarounds such as overuse of \u003ccode\u003eAsyncExitStack\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWe therefore propose to allow \u003ccode\u003ewith\u003c/code\u003e statements to accept both synchronous and asynchronous context managers in a single statement by prefixing individual async context managers with the \u003ccode\u003easync\u003c/code\u003e keyword.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis change eliminates unnecessary nesting, improves code readability, and improves ergonomics without making async code any less explicit.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003chr\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eFor example:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003easync def process_data():\n    async with acquire_lock() as lock:\n        with temp_directory() as tmpdir:\n            async with connect_to_db(cache=tmpdir) as db:\n                with open('config.json', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n                    # We're now 16 spaces deep before any actual logic\n                    config = json.load(f)\n                    await db.execute(config['query'])\n                    # ... more processing\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ebecomes\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003easync def process_data():\n    with (\n        async acquire_lock() as lock,\n        temp_directory() as tmpdir,\n        async connect_to_db(cache=tmpdir) as db,\n        open('config.json', encoding='utf-8') as f,\n    ):\n        config = json.load(f)\n        await db.execute(config['query'])\n        # ... more processing\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003caside class=\"onebox allowlistedgeneric\" data-onebox-src=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0806/\"\u003e\n  \u003cheader class=\"source\"\u003e\n      \u003cimg src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/3X/7/8/7808f673c94c5abc6cdd738548e39c7ae50dc418.png\" class=\"site-icon\" alt=\"\" data-dominant-color=\"979F6B\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\"\u003e\n\n      \u003ca href=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0806/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003ePython Enhancement Proposals (PEPs)\u003c/a\u003e\n  \u003c/header\u003e\n\n  \u003carticle class=\"onebox-body\"\u003e\n    \u003cimg width=\"200\" height=\"200\" src=\"https://us1.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/uploads/python1/original/3X/8/3/8354042ac932196b5fde1d1048a76b567decdcdc.png\" class=\"thumbnail onebox-avatar\" alt=\"\" data-dominant-color=\"9EAC81\"\u003e\n\n\u003ch3\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://peps.python.org/pep-0806/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003ePEP 806 – Mixed sync/async context managers with precise async marking |...\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/h3\u003e\n\n  \u003cp\u003ePython allows the with and async with statements to handle multiple context managers in a single statement, so long as they are all respectively synchronous or asynchronous.  When mixing synchronous and asynchronous context managers, developers must...\u003c/p\u003e\n\n\n  \u003c/article\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv class=\"onebox-metadata\"\u003e\n    \n    \n  \u003c/div\u003e\n\n  \u003cdiv style=\"clear: both\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n","post_number":1,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-09-25T17:54:46.405Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":589,"reads":257,"readers_count":256,"score":3556.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Zac Hatfield-Dodds","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://peps.python.org/pep-0806/","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"PEP 806 – Mixed sync/async context managers with precise async marking | peps.python.org","clicks":61},{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/allow-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-in-async-with-statements/92939","internal":true,"reflection":false,"title":"Allow mixed sync/async context managers in `async with` statements","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":36}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":447,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/1","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":36}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":36,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null,"can_vote":false},{"id":272290,"name":"jobe","username":"JoBe","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/jobe/{size}/26961_2.png","created_at":"2025-09-26T04:10:41.917Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI’m a big +1 on this, as I think this would help to improve the readability l. Are the parenthesis necessary however, or could work t be written as a one-liner?\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":2,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-09-26T04:10:41.917Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":221,"readers_count":220,"score":49.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"jobe","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":56933,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/2","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":272298,"name":"Zac Hatfield-Dodds","username":"Zac-HD","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/zac-hd/{size}/607_2.png","created_at":"2025-09-26T06:13:14.683Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThe PEP proposes allowing the one-line form, including a simple \u003ccode\u003ewith async ctx():\u003c/code\u003e without parentheses.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThat said, I think it will remain better style to write \u003ccode\u003easync with ctx():\u003c/code\u003e for this case, and would only \u003cem\u003erecommend\u003c/em\u003e using the new feature for multi-line parenthesized cases.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":3,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-09-26T06:13:14.683Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":2,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":214,"readers_count":213,"score":62.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Zac Hatfield-Dodds","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":56933,"username":"JoBe","name":"jobe","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/jobe/{size}/26961_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":447,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/3","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":272371,"name":"Carlos","username":"dr_carlos","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/dr_carlos/{size}/30461_2.png","created_at":"2025-09-26T22:44:48.505Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThe PEP only gives examples of\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003ewith (\n    async aquire_lock() as l,\n    open(\"file\") as f)\n):\n    ...\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eOr\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003ewith async aquire_lock() as l:\n    ...\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSo, to clarify, would\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cpre data-code-wrap=\"python\"\u003e\u003ccode class=\"lang-python\"\u003ewith async aquire_lock() as l, open(\"file\") as f:\n    ...\n\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/pre\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eBe allowed?\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":4,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-09-26T22:44:48.505Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":3,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":2,"reads":206,"readers_count":205,"score":71.2,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Carlos","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"","reply_to_user":{"id":447,"username":"Zac-HD","name":"Zac Hatfield-Dodds","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/zac-hd/{size}/607_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":70549,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/4","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":272516,"name":"Joegottman1","username":"joegottman1","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/joegottman1/{size}/31296_2.png","created_at":"2025-09-29T01:15:44.731Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI have a related question: What happens with \u003ccode\u003ewith async\u003c/code\u003e followed by parentheses. Is the code\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ccode\u003ewith async (  \u003c/code\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ccode\u003efoo_context() as foo,  \u003c/code\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\n\u003ccode\u003ebar_context() as bar ):\u003c/code\u003e\u003cbr\u003e\nlegal, and so are both foo and bar asynchronous or only foo.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eJoe Gottman\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":5,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-09-29T01:15:44.731Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":4,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":2,"reads":175,"readers_count":174,"score":65.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Joegottman1","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":70549,"username":"dr_carlos","name":"Carlos","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/dr_carlos/{size}/30461_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":75650,"hidden":false,"trust_level":0,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/5","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":272527,"name":"Zac Hatfield-Dodds","username":"Zac-HD","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/zac-hd/{size}/607_2.png","created_at":"2025-09-29T05:55:14.473Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThanks for asking!  The \u003ccode\u003easync\u003c/code\u003e keyword is specified in relation to the \u003ca href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.withitem\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\"\u003e\u003ccode\u003east.withitem\u003c/code\u003e\u003c/a\u003e, and so this would be a \u003ccode\u003eSyntaxError\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIf you play around with adding parens in the current \u003ccode\u003ewith\u003c/code\u003e statement, you’ll find that the only valid placements are (1) around the entire expression, \u003ccode\u003ewith ( …. ):\u003c/code\u003e, or around individual context-manager-returning expressions like \u003ccode\u003ewith ctx1() as a, (foo().ctx_attr) as b:\u003c/code\u003e.  The PEP doesn’t propose changing this, only allowing you to prepend \u003ccode\u003easync\u003c/code\u003e to each \u003ccode\u003east.withitem\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":6,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-09-29T05:55:14.473Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":5,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":2,"reads":172,"readers_count":171,"score":59.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Zac Hatfield-Dodds","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/ast.html#ast.withitem","internal":false,"reflection":false,"title":"ast — Abstract Syntax Trees — Python 3.13.7 documentation","clicks":3}],"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":75650,"username":"joegottman1","name":"Joegottman1","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/joegottman1/{size}/31296_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":447,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/6","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":272543,"name":"Tin Tvrtković","username":"Tinche","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/tinche/{size}/12507_2.png","created_at":"2025-09-29T08:38:20.492Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI’m +1 on the PEP!\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIt’s maybe a little unfortunate the motivating examples use file operations as examples of sync operations. I’ve already seen comments on Reddit to the tune of “hurr durr they should be using async wrappers for files instead“ (while a complex topic, also isn’t incorrect in the general sense). I imagine the main motivating cases are actually the Trio cancel scope managers and their ilk?\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eSome examples I’ve encountered where I’ve needed to mix sync and async context managers is using our internal library for tracing (\u003ccode\u003ewith TRACER.span():\u003c/code\u003e ) and the timeout managers in my Quattro library (which are modeled after the Trio ones). But I understand you can’t stick those into a PEP.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEdit: maybe examples using \u003ccode\u003epytest.raises(…)\u003c/code\u003e and \u003ccode\u003econtextlib.suppress(…)\u003c/code\u003e would be clearer.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":7,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-09-29T10:41:39.541Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":3,"reads":168,"readers_count":167,"score":63.6,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Tin Tvrtković","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":2,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":1820,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/7","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":273678,"name":"Akif","username":"yicopa3177","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/yicopa3177/{size}/31525_2.png","created_at":"2025-10-08T01:15:58.694Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI’m a bit skeptical of this proposal because it weakens Python’s principle that async code should be visually explicit. One of the best design decisions with async/await was making asynchronous code unmistakably distinct: when you see \u003ccode\u003easync with\u003c/code\u003e, you immediately know “this can suspend execution.” The proposal blurs this by allowing \u003ccode\u003ewith async ctx()\u003c/code\u003e which looks like a sync statement with an async detail buried inside. At a glance, you lose the clear signal that this block involves async operations. The current forced nesting actually serves a purpose: it makes you think about the async/sync boundary and makes it visually obvious which scopes are async.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe motivating example showing “16 spaces deep” is already a code smell that should be refactored regardless of async: you shouldn’t have four nested context managers in the first place. If deep nesting is genuinely necessary, the explicitness of separate \u003ccode\u003easync with\u003c/code\u003e blocks is worth the verbosity because it maintains clarity about where suspension points exist. In my view the ergonomic improvement isn’t worth compromising the visual distinction that makes async code safe and maintainable.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":8,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-10-08T01:16:06.550Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":4,"reads":142,"readers_count":141,"score":108.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Akif","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":4}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":76566,"hidden":false,"trust_level":1,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/8","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":4}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":4,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":274723,"name":"Michael H","username":"mikeshardmind","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/mikeshardmind/{size}/14381_2.png","created_at":"2025-10-16T15:18:19.652Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eI had to give this some significant thought and look at my own use, personal and professional.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI don’t personally see allowing mixing these as a positive after doing so.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI couldn’t find any examples in my own use that even came close to paralleling this, and I do think that this compromises on the primary benefit of async as a keyword in the first place (the high visibility and clear ordering of context switches, note that these context switches also happen on exiting the async context managers)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI’m going to critique the example, heavily, but the same critiques likely apply to any other code that would seem to benefit from this, as I believe there’s a reason I can’t find any code in my own use that would even remotely benefit from this.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe example given seems pretty weak, I’d like to see a realworld example where you actually need to aquire these, in this particular order, all at once, using a mix of sync and async context managers to do it. If the ordering is unimportant, just that all are aquired before continuing, then released afterward, or even if only the aquisition of the lock happening at the outermost level matters, you can already group this into two groups (those that are async, and those that are not).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWhat’s the lock even for? temp directories should always be sufficiently unique, file access can be in exclusive mode, and dbs typically provide their own locking as needed. If multiple things would be frequently updating the same config file, and this is intentional, you should be using a write queue rather than locked access, or even storing configuration in the database.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eEdit: I think that this is essentially already acting as a visual indication of code that should be refactored, not of code that the language needs to accomodate better.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":9,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-10-16T15:24:40.302Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":3,"reads":130,"readers_count":129,"score":76.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Michael H","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":2,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":2}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":15730,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/9","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":2}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":2,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":274788,"name":"Tin Tvrtković","username":"Tinche","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/tinche/{size}/12507_2.png","created_at":"2025-10-17T08:50:02.965Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"mikeshardmind\" data-post=\"9\" data-topic=\"103971\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/mikeshardmind/48/14381_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e mikeshardmind:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI couldn’t find any examples in my own use that even came close to paralleling this, and I do think that this compromises on the primary benefit of async as a keyword in the first place (the high visibility and clear ordering of context switches, note that these context switches also happen on exiting the async context managers)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eA simple question: are you using \u003ccode\u003easyncio.timeout()\u003c/code\u003e? If you value the benefit of explicit suspension points (like I do, and you imply you do) you should be using something else instead (either a home-grown solution, or \u003ccode\u003equattro.fail_after()\u003c/code\u003e/\u003ccode\u003equattro.move_on_after()\u003c/code\u003e and friends) since \u003ccode\u003easyncio.timeout()\u003c/code\u003e is an async context manager but without any possible suspension points inside. (Fun fact, I raised this point while working on it with Andrew but we were ultimately overruled.)\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eAnd I personally have had need to combine \u003ccode\u003equattro.fail_after()\u003c/code\u003e with taskgroups etc, so that would be my concrete example.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":10,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-10-17T08:50:02.965Z","reply_count":1,"reply_to_post_number":9,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":125,"readers_count":124,"score":45.0,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Tin Tvrtković","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":1}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":1820,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/10","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":1}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":1,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":274812,"name":"Michael H","username":"mikeshardmind","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/mikeshardmind/{size}/14381_2.png","created_at":"2025-10-17T14:25:40.478Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eNo. If I need a timeout on a coroutine that doesn’t provide it, I don’t use asyncio.timeout, but asyncio.wait_for or asyncio.wait (depending on the cancellation semantics needed in case of timeout) with a timeout parameter. While structured in code differently than the [edit: closest] context manager equivalent, the semantics are in general better.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":11,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2025-10-17T16:05:03.437Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":10,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":124,"readers_count":123,"score":24.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Michael H","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":2,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":1820,"username":"Tinche","name":"Tin Tvrtković","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/tinche/{size}/12507_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":15730,"hidden":false,"trust_level":3,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/11","reactions":[],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":0,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":291002,"name":"Pablo Galindo Salgado","username":"pablogsal","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/pablogsal/{size}/32246_2.png","created_at":"2026-04-23T18:27:52.586Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eOn behalf of the Python Steering Council: We’ve chosen to reject PEP 806, “Mixed sync/async context managers with precise async marking”. We thank Zac for the careful survey and write-up, and we acknowledge that the nesting pattern the PEP describes is a real annoyance for code that mixes sync and async context managers. However, the cost of adding new syntax to Python is very high: a permanent addition to the grammar is paid for by every parser, AST consumer, linter, formatter, type checker, and teaching resource in the ecosystem, and it is carried forward by every Python programmer from here on. The problem this PEP addresses, while genuine, does not in our view meet that bar. The PEP’s own survey suggests adoption would be limited to a specific subset of async code that also uses synchronous context managers, and that narrow payoff is not enough on its own to justify new syntax.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eIn addition to that background concern, we have several more specific reservations:\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe proposal introduces two valid spellings with different rules. Under this PEP, async with ctx(): and with async ctx(): would both be valid spellings of an async context manager entry, with different compositional rules: the former disallows mixing, the latter permits it. The PEP explicitly defers style enforcement to linters and formatters, but we are reluctant to ship syntax whose usability depends on external tooling to decide which of two legal forms is idiomatic.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eWe have some concerns about how with async interacts with the existing async marking convention. Today every line performing asynchronous work begins with async or await as its first non-whitespace token, which is a property readers and static analysis tools often rely on. Placing async as an item-level prefix inside a parenthesised with block changes that, and the PEP’s own recommendation to keep async with for single-line statements suggests the authors share some of this concern. On balance we would prefer to preserve the existing convention rather than loosen it for a syntactic convenience.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eExisting workarounds are adequate for the cases the PEP addresses. contextlib.AsyncExitStack and the as_acm() helper pattern already cover the mixed sync/async case. The PEP’s objection to as_acm() rests on the await sleep(0) cancellation checkpoint breaking the property that syntactic await/async for/async with always yields to the scheduler, but the PEP itself acknowledges that few codebases enforce this property today. We would be receptive to a proposal for a stdlib version of as_acm(), with or without the checkpoint, as a smaller and more targeted change.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eNone of this is to dismiss the underlying pain point. We would encourage interested parties to continue exploring library-level solutions, and we think the survey data gathered for this PEP would be valuable input into such a discussion. Thanks to Zac for the work, and to everyone who contributed to the discussion.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":12,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2026-04-23T18:27:52.586Z","reply_count":2,"reply_to_post_number":null,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":122,"reads":119,"readers_count":118,"score":813.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Pablo Galindo Salgado","primary_group_name":"committers","flair_name":"steering-council","flair_url":"fab-python","flair_bg_color":"FFE873","flair_color":"4B8BBE","flair_group_id":45,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"link_counts":[{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-828-supporting-yield-from-in-asynchronous-generators/106459/54","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"PEP 828: Supporting 'yield from' in asynchronous generators","clicks":1},{"url":"https://discuss.python.org/t/pep-828-supporting-yield-from-in-asynchronous-generators/106459/56","internal":true,"reflection":true,"title":"PEP 828: Supporting 'yield from' in asynchronous generators","clicks":0}],"read":true,"user_title":"Steering Council Member","title_is_group":false,"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":12}],"moderator":true,"admin":true,"staff":true,"user_id":4,"hidden":false,"trust_level":4,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/12","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":12}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":12,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":291045,"name":"Zac Hatfield-Dodds","username":"Zac-HD","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/zac-hd/{size}/607_2.png","created_at":"2026-04-24T04:16:33.983Z","cooked":"\u003cp\u003eThank you, Pablo and Steering Council, for your careful stewardship of Python!  I also appreciate the writeup of your reservations.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eI don’t think an \u003ccode\u003eas_acm()\u003c/code\u003e proposal is yet mature enough to propose for the stdlib or even third-party frameworks, but I’ll now spend some time experimenting with library-level solutions, and aim to write up any generally-useful observations or suggestions.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":13,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2026-04-24T04:16:33.983Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":12,"quote_count":0,"incoming_link_count":0,"reads":104,"readers_count":103,"score":50.8,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Zac Hatfield-Dodds","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":null,"reply_to_user":{"id":4,"username":"pablogsal","name":"Pablo Galindo Salgado","avatar_template":"/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/pablogsal/{size}/32246_2.png"},"bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":2}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":447,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/13","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":2}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":2,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_answer":false,"accepted_answer":false,"topic_accepted_answer":null},{"id":291055,"name":"Brendan Barnwell","username":"BrenBarn","avatar_template":"https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/b/74df32/{size}.png","created_at":"2026-04-24T08:07:34.299Z","cooked":"\u003caside class=\"quote group-committers\" data-username=\"pablogsal\" data-display-name=\"Pablo Galindo Salgado\" data-post=\"12\" data-topic=\"103971\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"title\"\u003e\n\u003cdiv class=\"quote-controls\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cimg alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://sea2.discourse-cdn.com/flex002/user_avatar/discuss.python.org/pablogsal/48/32246_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"\u003e Pablo Galindo Salgado:\u003c/div\u003e\n\u003cblockquote\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eToday every line performing asynchronous work begins with async or await as its first non-whitespace token, which is a property readers and static analysis tools often rely on.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/blockquote\u003e\n\u003c/aside\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eJust a nitpick since there are plenty of other reasons given, but this isn’t true.  \u003ccode\u003eawait\u003c/code\u003e can be used in expressions like \u003ccode\u003eblah = await foo\u003c/code\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e","post_number":14,"post_type":1,"posts_count":14,"updated_at":"2026-04-24T08:07:34.299Z","reply_count":0,"reply_to_post_number":12,"quote_count":1,"incoming_link_count":4,"reads":102,"readers_count":101,"score":100.4,"yours":false,"topic_id":103971,"topic_slug":"pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking","display_username":"Brendan Barnwell","primary_group_name":null,"flair_name":null,"flair_url":null,"flair_bg_color":null,"flair_color":null,"flair_group_id":null,"badges_granted":[],"version":1,"can_edit":false,"can_delete":false,"can_recover":false,"can_see_hidden_post":false,"can_wiki":false,"read":true,"user_title":"","bookmarked":false,"actions_summary":[{"id":2,"count":4}],"moderator":false,"admin":false,"staff":false,"user_id":5011,"hidden":false,"trust_level":2,"deleted_at":null,"user_deleted":false,"edit_reason":null,"can_view_edit_history":true,"wiki":false,"post_url":"/t/pep-806-mixed-sync-async-context-managers-with-precise-async-marking/103971/14","reactions":[{"id":"heart","type":"emoji","count":4}],"current_user_reaction":null,"reaction_users_count":4,"current_user_used_main_reaction":false,"can_accept_answer":false,"can_unaccept_a